{
  "id": "m44l1",
  "title": {
    "es": "Closures con estado y partial",
    "en": "Stateful closures and partial"
  },
  "theory": {
    "es": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Funciones que recuerdan"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "Una función interna puede modificar una variable de la externa con `nonlocal`: así mantiene **estado** entre llamadas (un acumulador, un contador…)."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "def acumulador():",
          "    total = 0",
          "    def add(x):",
          "        nonlocal total",
          "        total += x",
          "        return total",
          "    return add",
          "",
          "a = acumulador()",
          "print(a(10), a(5))"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "10 15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "`functools.partial` fija algunos argumentos de una función y devuelve otra con menos parámetros."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "from functools import partial",
          "def multiplicar(a, b):",
          "    return a * b",
          "doblar = partial(multiplicar, 2)",
          "print(doblar(5))"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "value": "partial es como dejar 'preconfigurada' una función para no repetir siempre el mismo argumento."
      }
    ],
    "en": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Functions that remember"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "An inner function can modify a variable of the outer one with `nonlocal`: that's how it keeps **state** between calls (an accumulator, a counter…)."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "def acumulador():",
          "    total = 0",
          "    def add(x):",
          "        nonlocal total",
          "        total += x",
          "        return total",
          "    return add",
          "",
          "a = acumulador()",
          "print(a(10), a(5))"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "10 15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "`functools.partial` fixes some arguments of a function and returns another with fewer parameters."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "from functools import partial",
          "def multiplicar(a, b):",
          "    return a * b",
          "doblar = partial(multiplicar, 2)",
          "print(doblar(5))"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "value": "partial is like leaving a function 'preconfigured' so you don't repeat the same argument."
      }
    ]
  },
  "exercises": [
    {
      "id": "m44l1e1",
      "prompt": {
        "es": "Define `acumulador()` que devuelva una función que SUMA cada valor recibido y devuelve el total acumulado.",
        "en": "Define `acumulador()` returning a function that ADDS each received value and returns the running total."
      },
      "starter_code": [
        "def acumulador():",
        "    pass"
      ],
      "solution": [
        "def acumulador():",
        "    total = 0",
        "    def add(x):",
        "        nonlocal total",
        "        total += x",
        "        return total",
        "    return add"
      ],
      "hints": {
        "es": [
          "Usa nonlocal para modificar total dentro de la función interna."
        ],
        "en": [
          "Use nonlocal to modify total inside the inner function."
        ]
      },
      "tests": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "name": "acumulador",
          "harness": "a = acumulador()\nassert a(10) == 10\nassert a(5) == 15\nassert a(100) == 115\nb = acumulador()\nassert b(1) == 1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "m44l1e2",
      "prompt": {
        "es": "Define `multiplicar(a, b)` y, con `partial`, una función `triple` que multiplique por 3.",
        "en": "Define `multiplicar(a, b)` and, with `partial`, a function `triple` that multiplies by 3."
      },
      "starter_code": [
        "from functools import partial",
        "",
        "def multiplicar(a, b):",
        "    return a * b",
        "",
        "# define triple con partial"
      ],
      "solution": [
        "from functools import partial",
        "",
        "def multiplicar(a, b):",
        "    return a * b",
        "",
        "triple = partial(multiplicar, 3)"
      ],
      "hints": {
        "es": [
          "triple = partial(multiplicar, 3)."
        ],
        "en": [
          "triple = partial(multiplicar, 3)."
        ]
      },
      "tests": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "name": "triple",
          "harness": "assert triple(5) == 15\nassert triple(0) == 0"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}